e as incumbent in an 8-point-Democratic state"], "rationale": "Every recent poll shows Hochul leading, with the two best-regarded surveys in late September at +9 and +19, and averages cluster around 8-10 points. Markets and raters agree she is a strong favorite, but the single-digit Siena result and headlines about a slipping lead argue for the lower end of the polling range rather than a double-digit margin.", "watch": "Whether Blakeman can consolidate Republican-leaning voters and keep the race in single digits, as the Siena poll suggests, or whether cost-of-living discontent translates into a late shift.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Incumbent Kathy Hochul (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Incumbent Democrat Kathy Hochul seeks a full term against Nassau County Republican Bruce Blakeman in a state that leaned Democratic by eight points at the last presidential election. Polling through late September consistently shows Hochul ahead: Quinnipiac put her up 19 among likely voters, while Siena's concurrent survey had a tighter 9-point race, and earlier polls ranged from 4 to 20 points. Averages of the polls sit around 8 to 10 points in her favor.\n\nBetting markets give Hochul roughly a 94-95% chance, and every major race rating, from Cook to Sabato, places the contest in the Safe or Solid Democratic column. Fundraising is roughly comparable, with Hochul holding a small cash-on-hand edge. The main question is whether the race stays comfortably in Hochul's column or tightens into single digits in the final weeks.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9859, "margin": 12.77}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9584, "margin": 9.0}]}